# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Copy to `.env` — `cp .env.example .env` — and fill in AUTH_SECRET.
#  Everything else already works as it stands for local development.
#  `.env` is git-ignored; this file is the one that is committed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The MySQL server, read by both the app (lib/db.ts) and the Prisma CLI
# (prisma.config.ts). Create the database first — Prisma migrates into it but
# will not create it for you:
#
#   mysql -u root -p -e "CREATE DATABASE mizmar_school
#     CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_unicode_ci;"
#
# utf8mb4 is not optional: the app stores Arabic throughout, and the collation
# is what makes a French-language search case- and accent-insensitive.
DATABASE_URL="mysql://root:root@localhost:3306/mizmar_school"

# The certificate authority that signed the database server's certificate, for
# a managed MySQL whose root is not one Node already trusts (Aiven, and most
# hosts that issue a per-project CA). Leave it unset for a local server or one
# with a publicly-trusted certificate; setting it is what turns the connection
# into a *verified* TLS connection rather than merely an encrypted one.
#
# Take it from the provider's console — Aiven puts it under "CA certificate" on
# the service overview — and give it either as the PEM itself or, because most
# deployment consoles mangle a multi-line value, base64-encoded:
#
#   node -e "console.log(fs.readFileSync('ca.pem').toString('base64'))"
#
# Do not put `?ssl-mode=REQUIRED` on DATABASE_URL — that is a `mysql` client
# flag; the driver does not read it, and this variable is what it reads instead.
# DATABASE_SSL_CA=""

# Signs the session cookie (Auth.js) and the native app's bearer tokens
# (lib/mobile-token.ts) — one secret, both paths. Changing it signs everybody
# out. Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
AUTH_SECRET=""

# ── Optional: the seed ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by all three seeds (prisma/seed.ts, seed-config.ts, seed-empty.ts).
# Set these before the *first* seed of a real deployment; the seeds upsert on
# the email, so changing it later adds a second administrator rather than
# renaming the first.

# The super administrator's email. Staff sign in with the local part of it —
# `admin@groupescolaire.ma` means signing in as `admin`.
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@groupescolaire.ma"

# The password every seeded account gets. Change it for anything but a demo.
# SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD="Admin123!"

# The organisation's name, for `npm run db:seed:empty`. Everything else on the
# organisation — ICE, tax id, address, telephone — is filled in on
# /organization, because those head every receipt and a plausible wrong one is
# worse than a blank.
# SEED_ORG_NAME="Votre établissement"

# ── Optional: development ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `PRISMA_LOG_QUERIES=1 npm run dev` prints every SQL statement. Off by default:
# it is one line per query and the dashboard alone issues fifty. Ignored outside
# NODE_ENV=development.
# PRISMA_LOG_QUERIES=0
